here are, however, unflagged first-person claims:\n\n> \u201cI don't build AI workflows around a single model provider anymore\u2026\u201d\n\nThis is traceable almost verbatim to the previous site copy, so it does not look fabricated. But the Hard Rules explicitly say prior-site claims still require user review and flagging ([product-marketing.md](</Users/rodneylewis/Sprintz/Internal/rl22.github.io/.claude/worktrees/unhide-homepage-sections-b06d72/.agents/product-marketing.md:65>)).\n\n> \u201cI want the smallest boundary that keeps task logic separate from a volatile dependency\u2026\u201d\n\nThat first-person operating preference was introduced in the rewrite rather than stated verbatim in the previous body. It should be flagged for Rodney\u2019s approval.\n\n## Factual accuracy\n\nThe two factual citations are accurate:\n\n- OpenAI does say behavior can change between snapshots and recommends pinned versions plus evals. The existing URL now redirects to the newer canonical documentation, so the claim is sound but the link should be updated. [OpenAI API compatibility](https://developers.openai.com/api/reference/overview#backwards-compatibility).\n- Bedrock currently uses Active, Legacy, and End-of-Life lifecycle states and provides transition guidance. [Amazon Bedrock model lifecycle](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/model-lifecycle.html).\n\nThis is a Thoughts essay, not a repo review. It makes no claims about GitHub repository structures, file paths, or commands. There is therefore nothing repo-specific to disprove; the problem is the omitted LiteLLM example the brief required.\n\n## Prose problems\n\n> \u201cThese are normal platform conditions, not edge cases.\u201d\n\nThis is a generic certainty statement. The citations support model lifecycle and behavior drift, but not every preceding risk as a \u201cnormal\u201d condition. Narrow it or remove it.\n\n> \u201cA provider may return \u2026 a valid response that no longer behaves like the version you evaluated.\u201d\n\nResponses do not \u201cbehave\u201d; models or outputs do. This should say that a successful response may contain output that no longer matches the behavior previously evaluated.\n\n> \u201c\u2026anyone doing serious engineering work with AI tools should think twice\u2026\u201d\n\n\u201cSerious engineering work\u201d is gatekeeping throat-clearing rather than evidence. It weakens the otherwise peer-to-peer voice.\n\n## What passes plainly\n\n- Exact required H2 order.\n- Four task examples and routing matrix.\n- Operational risks, adapter/config, abstraction costs, and direct-SDK exception.\n- Both exact asset placeholders in the correct positions.\n- Discipline values and ordering.\n- 1,235 words by the repository\u2019s raw body-count method, within the 1,100\u20131,300 target.\n- No frontmatter, H1, bare body URLs, testimonials, or invented repo commands.", "escalated_from": null}
